  title="Repaint or Touch Up Between Tenants? How to Decide"
  description="When a touch up is enough at a rental turnover, when a full repaint is the better call, and the whole wall approach that often gives the best result."
>
<p>Touch up the paint between tenants when it is less than about two or three years old, you have the same product in the same sheen, and the marks are limited to a few spots per wall. Repaint when the paint is older, when patches and scuffs are spread across most walls, when there is smoke or cooking residue, or when you do not know exactly what is on the walls now. There is also a middle path that fits many turnovers: repaint only the walls that need it, corner to corner, and leave the rest alone. A simple test settles most cases. Touch up one spot, let it dry, and look at it from the side in daylight. If you can find it, a prospective tenant will too.</p>

<h2>Why touch ups sometimes show</h2>
<p>A touch up is supposed to disappear. When it does not, one of four things is usually the reason.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sheen.</strong> Paint on a wall slowly loses some of its sheen as it ages and gets cleaned. Fresh paint over it reflects light a little differently, even from the same can. Painters call this flashing. It shows most when you look along the wall toward a window.</li>
<li><strong>Color drift.</strong> Paint fades with sunlight and time. Two batches of the same color can also differ slightly. The longer the paint has been on the wall, the more a fresh patch stands out.</li>
<li><strong>Texture.</strong> A wall that was rolled has a fine stipple. A brushed touch up is smooth. The color can match perfectly and the patch still catches the eye.</li>
<li><strong>Unprimed patches.</strong> Spackle and joint compound absorb paint differently than the painted wall around them. A patched nail hole that is painted without primer often shows as a dull spot.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these is manageable when you know what you are working with.</p>

<h2>When a touch up is the right call</h2>
<ul>
<li>The paint is recent, generally within the last two or three years.</li>
<li>You have leftover paint from the same job, or you know the exact product, color and sheen.</li>
<li>The finish is flat or matte, which hides touch ups best.</li>
<li>The marks are small and few: nail holes, a scuff behind a door, a chair mark.</li>
<li>The walls were not exposed to smoke or heavy cooking.</li>
</ul>
<p>Technique matters as much as the paint. Clean the spot first. Spot prime any patch. Use a small roller with the same nap as the original job, not a brush. Use as little paint as possible, and feather the edges outward so there is no hard line.</p>

<h2>When a full repaint is the right call</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The paint has reached its age.</strong> Interior paint in a rental generally needs a full repaint every three to five years, or about every other turn. Our article on <a href="/articles/how-long-rental-finishes-last/">how long rental finishes last</a> has typical lifespans for paint and other surfaces.</li>
<li><strong>There are many patches.</strong> A wall with a dozen filled holes is faster to repaint than to touch up, and it looks better.</li>
<li><strong>There is smoke, grease or odor.</strong> These need cleaning and a stain blocking primer before any finish coat. Spot work will not cover them evenly.</li>
<li><strong>The color or sheen is unknown.</strong> Without the original product, a match is a guess.</li>
<li><strong>You are changing the color.</strong> For example, moving a property to your standard color.</li>
<li><strong>The finish is satin or semi gloss on the walls.</strong> Higher sheens show every touch up.</li>
<li><strong>A previous tenant painted.</strong> Accent walls and partial repaints usually need primer and two coats to return to the standard.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The middle path: paint whole walls</h2>
<p>Between dabbing at spots and repainting the entire unit, there is an option that often gives the best value at a turnover. Paint the walls that need it from corner to corner and from ceiling to baseboard, and leave the walls that are in good shape.</p>
<p>This works because a change in plane hides small differences. Two walls that meet at a corner are never lit the same way, so a slight shift in sheen or color between them is not visible. A patch in the middle of a wall is.</p>
<p>The walls that typically need it are the ones with the most contact: hallways, the wall behind the sofa, the wall by the entry, kids' rooms, and the area around light switches. Closets, ceilings and low traffic bedrooms often carry over for another turn with no work at all.</p>

<h2>Walk the unit in this order</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Find out how old the paint is.</strong> Your property file or the last invoice should have the date and the product.</li>
<li><strong>Clean first.</strong> Many marks are dirt, not damage. A melamine sponge or mild cleaner removes a good share of scuffs. Decide what needs paint after the walls are clean.</li>
<li><strong>Count the repairs per wall.</strong> One to three small spots is touch up territory. More than that, plan to paint the wall.</li>
<li><strong>Check for stains and odor.</strong> Water stains, smoke, grease near the stove, and marker or crayon all need a stain blocking primer.</li>
<li><strong>Do the test spot.</strong> Touch up one mark in a less visible place, let it dry completely, and look at it from an angle in daylight.</li>
<li><strong>Decide room by room.</strong> It is common to repaint the living room and hallway, paint two walls in a bedroom, and touch up the rest.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Trim, doors and ceilings</h2>
<p><strong>Trim and doors</strong> are usually semi gloss, which cleans up well. Wash them before deciding anything. Baseboards and door casings in high traffic areas take the most wear, and repainting a full length of baseboard or a whole door looks better than spot work on a glossy surface.</p>
<p><strong>Ceilings</strong> usually last longer than walls and can be left alone at most turns. The exceptions are stains from a past leak, which need primer and paint after the cause has been fixed, and kitchens or baths where steam and cooking have dulled the finish.</p>

<h2>What makes touch ups work for years</h2>
<p>Owners who rarely need full repaints tend to have a few habits in common.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One standard.</strong> The same color, the same product line and the same sheen in every unit. A touch up in one property matches the paint in the next.</li>
<li><strong>A record.</strong> Brand, product, color name or formula, sheen and the date, kept in the property file.</li>
<li><strong>Labeled leftover paint.</strong> A sealed quart from each job, marked with the address and date, stored somewhere it will not freeze.</li>
<li><strong>A washable wall finish.</strong> A scrubbable matte or eggshell lets you clean marks off instead of painting over them.</li>
<li><strong>Clear move in documentation.</strong> Photos of the walls at move in make move out decisions quick and fair.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How paint fits into the turnover schedule</h2>
<p>Paint comes after repairs and patching and before final cleaning and carpet work. A touch up package takes hours. A full interior repaint of a typical unit takes a day or two, plus drying time. Painting whole walls falls in between. Knowing which one you need before the tenant moves out lets the work start the day the unit is empty. Our <a href="/articles/rental-make-ready-checklist/">rental make ready checklist</a> lays out the full sequence.</p>

<h2>Paint and the security deposit</h2>
<p>Paint wears out with normal living. Minor scuffs, a reasonable number of small nail holes and gentle fading are ordinary wear in most cases. Large holes, unapproved colors, heavy smoke residue and drawings on the walls go beyond that. The age of the paint matters too. A wall that was due for repainting anyway is treated differently than one painted six months ago. Our article on <a href="/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/">wear and tear versus damage in Tennessee</a> explains where those lines fall and how to document them. For anything involving a deduction, your lease and your attorney are the right sources.</p>

<h2>What a prospective tenant sees</h2>
<p>Walls are the largest surface in any room, and they set the first impression at a showing and in listing photos. People rarely notice walls that look clean and even. They do notice a patchwork of slightly different squares. The goal of either approach is the same: walls that look uniform from the doorway. If touch ups get you there, they are the efficient choice. If they do not, painting the wall is the better use of the day.</p>

